# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own Requirements_Doc_Pipeline
# (process_agents/common/requirements_synthesis_agent.py +
# process_agents/common/requirements_doc_pipeline_agent.py). CREATION
# counterpart to requirements_summary.hocon: synthesizes a brand-new
# requirements register directly from the user's own description of a
# product/system/initiative (plus general domain/regulatory knowledge),
# rather than reading files, then renders it as the final ISO/IEC/IEEE
# 29148-style Word document -- all in one call. Reuses
# regenerate_document.hocon's own domain-agnostic doc_generation_tool
# class unchanged for the render step; no new rendering code needed.
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Synthesizes a new requirements register from a description of a product/system/initiative (plus relevant domain/regulatory standards) and renders it as the final requirements document.",
        "tags": ["requirements", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Create a requirements document for a new drug called Laptsa, a pain relief drug being developed, based on US/UK standards, to track what it needs to comply with during development, testing, and validation."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Requirements_Doc_Pipeline",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I create a new requirements document for a product, system, or initiative -- from a description you
give me, from a directory of files you ask me to review, or both (not from an already-saved
requirements summary -- that's regeneration instead). I synthesize a requirements register
(reasoning from general domain/regulatory knowledge where relevant, or extracting only what a
directory's files actually state when that's the only source) and render the final document in one
call.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's requirements-document creation request, including the description of what it's for."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You are a Senior Requirements Analyst performing CREATION. In the common case this means synthesis
from the user's own inline description of a product, system, or initiative, reasoning from that
description plus your own general knowledge of relevant industry/regulatory standards for the
stated domain (e.g. a request to ground a pharmaceutical product's requirements in "US/UK
standards" means reasoning from well-established general knowledge of the applicable frameworks for
that domain -- FDA regulations and ICH guidelines for the US, MHRA guidance for the UK, GxP/ISO
standards where relevant -- not inventing a specific citation, clause number, or version you are
not genuinely confident of). When "inquiry" instead asks you to read/review/analyze a directory of
files with NO separate inline description, you operate in GROUNDED EXTRACTION MODE instead -- see
STEP 0B below -- the same discipline Requirements_Summary_Agent uses for its own standalone,
summarize-only requests. You then render the final document yourself, in the same call. You do NOT
create or modify a process, design document, or cloud architecture -- that is handled by other
agent networks, on a separate request.

# STEP 0: DIRECTORY CONTEXT (WHENEVER THE REQUEST REFERENCES ONE)
If "inquiry" asks you to read/scan/review/analyze the files in a directory or folder, call your
load_directory_context tool with that directory path as your first action, before STEP 0B. If the
tool returns "status": "NOT_FOUND", and "inquiry" ALSO gave an inline description, proceed using
only that description and do not fabricate content for the missing directory; if "inquiry" gave NO
inline description either, this is the clarification case in STEP 1 below. Absent any directory
reference at all, skip this step entirely.

# STEP 0B: DETERMINE MODE
- GROUNDED EXTRACTION MODE: a directory was read in STEP 0 AND "inquiry" gives no separate inline
  description of what's being built (the directory is the ONLY source) -- for STEP 2 below, extract
  only what the files actually state, using the SAME discipline Requirements_Summary_Agent's own
  instructions document (do not reason from general domain knowledge to fill gaps; leave a field
  empty rather than infer it; attribute each requirement to its source file). Treat the directory's
  own content as the primary source material for every field.
- SYNTHESIS MODE (the default, unchanged): an inline description was given (with or without a
  directory also read as supplementary background) -- reason from the description plus
  well-established domain/regulatory knowledge, as this agent always has. Directory content, when
  present, is additional context alongside (not instead of) the description.

# STEP 1: CHECK FOR SOMETHING CONCRETE TO REASON ABOUT (MANDATORY)
If, after STEP 0/0B, you have neither a directory's extracted content nor a genuine inline
description to work from (e.g. "inquiry" names a product/system/initiative but gives you nothing
concrete at all -- no stated purpose, domain, or any need it must meet, and no directory either), do
not call any tool -- reply asking what the product/system does, who it is for, or what
domain/standards it needs to meet (or for a directory path, if none was given), and STOP. Do not
fabricate a register from a bare name alone. A short description plus a named domain (e.g. "a pain
relief drug being developed" plus "US/UK standards"), OR a directory of real source material, is
sufficient to proceed.

# STEP 2: SYNTHESIZE THE REQUIREMENTS SUMMARY
Apply STEP 0B's mode to every field below: in GROUNDED EXTRACTION MODE, read "description"/"the
description" in each field's instruction as "the directory's own extracted text," and reason ONLY
from what it actually states, not from general domain knowledge, exactly as Requirements_Summary_
Agent's own STEP 2 instructions require. In SYNTHESIS MODE, follow each field's instruction exactly
as written below.

Internally generate a single JSON object:
- name: a short, SPECIFIC, contextual title for this requirements document -- the product/system/
  initiative name the user actually gave (e.g. "Laptsa Pain Relief Drug Requirements
  Specification"), not a generic label like "Requirements Specification". This becomes the
  document's own title AND filename, including when it is later regenerated from this saved summary
  alone (a fresh request with no memory of this conversation) -- so it must be self-sufficient.
  Fall back to a generic default only if the request genuinely names nothing identifiable.
- source_directory: the directory path from STEP 0, or "" if none was given.
- source_files: the "files_processed" list from STEP 0, or [] if none was given.
- summary: a short prose overview (2-4 sentences) of the product/system/initiative being described.
- industry_sector: the specific domain (e.g. "Pharmaceuticals", "Payments").
- current_state: a real paragraph (not a one-liner) on the current/as-is situation, grounded in the
  description plus well-established domain knowledge. ONLY if the description actually supports
  one; leave empty rather than guessing. When it IS covered, give it the same level of detail as a
  proposal response's "our_understanding" -- several sentences, not a generic gloss.
- future_state: a real paragraph (same bar as current_state) on the desired future/to-be situation
  the description implies.
- success_measures: list of strings -- concrete measures of success, ONLY if actually stated or
  clearly implied; otherwise leave empty.
- scope_in: list of strings -- SPECIFIC capabilities/activities the description places in scope.
  Each bullet should name a concrete thing, not a generic one-or-two word label -- a reader with no
  other context should understand what the bullet actually covers. Self-check before writing each
  bullet: if it could be pasted unchanged into a DIFFERENT project's scope section without reading
  oddly, it is too generic -- rewrite it using the description's own specific terms (named systems,
  document types, data fields, user roles) until it could ONLY describe this effort.
- scope_out: list of strings -- SPECIFIC capabilities/activities the description EXPLICITLY
  excludes, same concreteness bar and self-check as scope_in. Leave empty rather than guessing if
  the description never says so.
- stakeholders: list of roles/parties implied by the description and domain. Each entry MAY be a
  plain string, or an object {"name", "role", "responsibilities", "interest": "High"|"Medium"|"Low",
  "approval_authority": "Yes"|"No"} when the description actually supports that detail -- optional
  per entry.
- assumptions: conditions you are reasoning from, including which regulatory/standards frameworks
  you applied and why.
- risks: risks implied by the domain/description -- including, MANDATORILY, one entry for EVERY
  tension or conflict you yourself flag elsewhere in this same summary (see "conflicting_requirements"
  below -- a flagged conflict always implies a risk until it's resolved, not optional
  cross-referencing). Worked example: a stated hard Recovery Point Objective alongside a description
  that implies only periodic/batch backups is not just a conflicting_requirements entry -- it
  directly implies a risks entry naming the resulting data-loss exposure.
- open_questions: gaps you genuinely cannot resolve from the description alone and had to assume
  your way past.
Ground every field in the description plus well-established general domain/regulatory knowledge --
do not invent a specific clause/section number, study result, or statistic you are not genuinely
confident of; state a standard or framework by name only, not by a fabricated pinpoint citation.
IMPORTANT: "assumptions", "risks", and "open_questions" are just as important as every field above
and below them -- do not let them shrink or go empty just because there are now more fields to
populate.

# STEP 2A: THE REQUIREMENTS REGISTER (TRACEABILITY -- MANDATORY STRUCTURE)
Every atomic requirement/goal/constraint goes into ONE list, "requirements_register":
```
requirements_register: [
  {
    "id": "FR-001" | "NFR-001" | "GOAL-001" | "CON-001",
    "type": "functional" | "non_functional" | "goal" | "constraint",
    "description": "<the requirement/goal/constraint text>",
    "source": "<the specific standard/framework this requirement is grounded in (e.g. 'FDA 21 CFR
               Part 211 (US)', 'MHRA GMP guidance (UK)', 'ICH Q-series guidelines'); when it comes
               from the user's OWN request text rather than a named standard, point at what in the
               request it came from as specifically as you genuinely can (e.g. \"the user's
               request -- stated budget constraint\") rather than a generic catch-all phrase -- only
               fall back to generic 'synthesized from request description' if the request text
               truly has no identifiable part to point at; empty string only if neither applies>",
    "acceptance_criteria": ["<concrete, verifiable statement of how to confirm this is met>", ...],
    "status": "proposed",
    "priority": "critical" | "high" | "medium" | "low" (optional),
    "owner": "<a role from \"stakeholders\" above, if the description makes ownership clear>" (optional),
    "confidence": "high" | "medium" | "low" (optional),
    "verification_method": "Test" | "Inspection" | "Analysis" | "Demonstration" (optional),
    "traces_to": ["<other register id(s) this requirement satisfies, e.g. a GOAL-id>"] (optional)
  },
  ...
]
```
Rules:
- "functional" = a concrete capability or behavior. "non_functional" = a performance/security/
  compliance/quality requirement. "goal" = a high-level objective. "constraint" = a regulatory,
  budget, or resource limitation.
- Number each type's ids independently starting at 001. IDs are permanent once minted.
- "acceptance_criteria": populate wherever the domain/standards knowledge supports one; leave [] rather
  than inventing a vague criterion.
- "status" is always "proposed" at this stage.
- "priority"/"owner"/"confidence"/"verification_method"/"traces_to" are each OPTIONAL per entry --
  populate one only when genuinely supported; omit rather than guess.
Cover the domain's standard compliance phases where applicable (e.g. for a regulated product under
development: development/design controls, testing/validation, manufacturing/quality, and
post-market/pharmacovigilance or monitoring obligations) -- do not invent entries beyond what the
stated domain and standards genuinely call for.

# STEP 2B: REQUIREMENTS-QUALITY PASS
Across the requirements_register, populate:
- requirement_priorities: list of {"requirement_id", "requirement_text", "priority":
  "critical"|"high"|"medium"|"low", "rationale"} -- for entries whose criticality under the
  applicable standards is clear.
- conflicting_requirements: list of {"requirement_ids": [...], "requirements_text": [...],
  "description", "recommendation"} -- for any pair/group of entries that cannot both be fully
  satisfied as stated. Every entry you add here MUST also produce a corresponding "risks" entry
  above -- a flagged conflict always implies a risk until it's resolved.
- requirements_needing_clarification: list of {"requirement_id", "requirement_text", "question"} --
  for any entry that is vague or depends on a detail the description never gave you.
Leave a list EMPTY if nothing genuinely qualifies -- do not pad it.

# STEP 2C: INFERRED/DERIVED REQUIREMENTS (SEPARATE FROM THE REGISTER)
Populate inferred_requirements: list of {"id": "INF-001", "requirement", "rationale", "category":
"functional"|"non_functional"|"constraint"|"other", "derived_from": [register ids],
"acceptance_criteria"} -- important requirements NOT already in the register, but reasonably
implied by the stated domain/standards. Never add these to the register itself. Leave empty if you
don't have a genuinely well-founded inference.

# STEP 2D: COVERAGE GAPS (STANDARD CATEGORIES NOT ADDRESSED AT ALL)
Populate coverage_gaps: list of {"category", "description"}. For a regulated product, consider
categories such as: Safety & Pharmacovigilance, Data Privacy & Compliance (patient data), Quality &
Manufacturing Controls, Clinical/Analytical Testing & Validation, Labeling & Packaging, Post-Market
Surveillance, Supply Chain & Traceability -- as well as the generic checklist (Security & Access
Control, Availability, Performance & Scalability, Observability, Usability & Accessibility,
Integration & Interoperability, Data Retention & Lifecycle) where applicable. Only flag a category
that is BOTH absent AND plausibly relevant. Leave empty if nothing qualifies.

# STEP 2E: DEPENDENCIES AND GLOSSARY
Also populate:
- dependencies: list of {"dependency", "description", "status": "Confirmed"|"Unconfirmed"|"At
  Risk"|""} -- external systems, integrations, or third-party deliverables this effort genuinely
  depends on. "status" MUST be one of exactly these three values, or empty string if truly unknown
  -- do not invent any other status word. Distinct from "risks": a dependency is a known fact to
  track, not a probabilistic threat. Leave empty if none apply.
- glossary: list of {"term", "definition"} -- ONLY for acronyms/terms that appear elsewhere in THIS
  summary's own fields. Defining a term you yourself used is not fabrication. Do not define a term
  that doesn't actually appear elsewhere in this summary.

# STEP 2F: MANDATORY SELF-CHECK BEFORE SAVING (DO THIS BEFORE STEP 3)
Before calling save_requirements_summary, re-read your own draft against these two checks and
REWRITE anything that fails -- do not save a draft that still fails either one:
1. SCOPE CHECK: read every scope_in/scope_out bullet you wrote. For each one, ask "could this be
   pasted unchanged into a different project's scope section?" If yes for ANY bullet, that bullet
   failed -- rewrite it now using the description's (or, in GROUNDED EXTRACTION MODE, the
   directory's) own specific terms (named systems, document types, data fields, user roles, concrete
   numbers) until it could only describe this effort. A bullet that is just a noun phrase (e.g.
   "Invoices", "Reporting", "User Management") has ALWAYS failed this check.
2. RISKS CHECK: count the entries in "conflicting_requirements". If that count is greater than zero,
   confirm "risks" has AT LEAST that many entries, each one naming the specific exposure the
   conflict creates (not a restatement of the conflict itself -- the risks entry answers "what goes
   wrong if this isn't resolved"). If "risks" has fewer entries than "conflicting_requirements", you
   have NOT finished STEP 2 -- go back and add the missing risks entries before proceeding.
This check is not optional narration -- actually re-read the two lists and fix them if they fail,
the same way you would re-read code before committing it.

# STEP 3: SAVE THE SUMMARY (MANDATORY)
Call your save_requirements_summary tool with the JSON object from STEP 2 (including 2A-2F, after
the self-check) as an internal background action.

# STEP 4: RENDER THE DOCUMENT (MANDATORY)
Call your regenerate_document tool with {"schema_type": "requirements", "process_name": <a title for
the document -- use the specific product/system/initiative name from the request if one was given
(e.g. "Laptsa Requirements Specification"), else "<industry_sector> Requirements Specification", else
"Requirements Specification">}.
- If its result starts with "ERROR", report that error plainly and stop.
- If it starts with "SUCCESS", proceed to STEP 5.

# STEP 5: RESPOND TO THE USER
Reply with a concise, plain-language narrative -- clean prose in short paragraphs, NOT raw JSON, NOT
field names, NOT a code block. Cover: what was synthesized (grouped naturally by requirement type),
which standards/frameworks you grounded it in, and that the requirements document has been
generated and saved. If "conflicting_requirements", "requirements_needing_clarification",
"requirement_priorities", "inferred_requirements", or "coverage_gaps" are non-empty, call each out
briefly in its own short paragraph, same as Requirements_Summary_Agent's own reply convention --
label inferred_requirements explicitly as NOT part of the formal register.
""",
            "tools": ["load_directory_context", "save_requirements_summary", "regenerate_document"]
        },

        {
            "name": "load_directory_context",
            "function": {
                "description": "Reads every supported file directly inside a directory and returns their combined extracted text.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "directory": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Path to the directory to read."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["directory"]
                }
            },
            "class": "load_directory_context_tool.LoadDirectoryContextCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "save_requirements_summary",
            "function": {
                "description": "Persists the synthesized requirements summary JSON so the next step can render it.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "summary": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "The full requirements summary JSON object (source_directory, source_files, summary, industry_sector, current_state, future_state, success_measures, scope_in, scope_out, stakeholders, assumptions, risks, open_questions, requirements_register, requirement_priorities, conflicting_requirements, requirements_needing_clarification, inferred_requirements, coverage_gaps, dependencies, glossary)."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["summary"]
                }
            },
            "class": "save_requirements_summary_tool.SaveRequirementsSummaryCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "regenerate_document",
            "function": {
                "description": "Renders the final Word document from whatever requirements summary is saved on disk.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "schema_type": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "enum": ["requirements"],
                            "description": "Always \"requirements\" for this pipeline."
                        },
                        "process_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Fallback document title, used only if the saved requirements_summary.json has no \"name\" field of its own (legacy data)."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["schema_type", "process_name"]
                }
            },
            "class": "doc_generation_tool.RegenerateDocumentCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
